> Quadratic gap contraction needs a separate connection to π

For positive a and b, let a'=(a+b)/2 and b'=sqrt(ab). Their exact gap satisfies

$$
a'-b'=\frac{(\sqrt a-\sqrt b)^2}{2}
       =\frac{(a-b)^2}{2(\sqrt a+\sqrt b)^2}.
$$
\alttext(The new gap is the square of the old gap divided by twice the squared sum of the square roots.)

As both approach a positive common limit L, the denominator approaches 8L.
The gap therefore contracts quadratically in exact arithmetic. This alone does
not determine π: its connection also uses the correction terms and Legendre's
relation for elliptic integrals. With the stated initial values, the update is

$$
\begin{aligned}
a_{n+1}&=(a_n+b_n)/2,&b_{n+1}&=\sqrt{a_nb_n},\\
t_{n+1}&=t_n-p_n(a_n-a_{n+1})^2,&p_{n+1}&=2p_n,\\
\widehat\pi_n&=(a_n+b_n)^2/(4t_n).
\end{aligned}
$$
\alttext(Update the two means, subtract the weighted squared mean change from t, double p, and estimate pi from the squared sum of the means divided by four t.)

[Brent (1976), Fast Multiple-Precision Evaluation of Elementary Functions](https://doi.org/10.1145/321941.321944),
Section 5, printed 246, gives the limit identity π=A²/T, where A is the common
AGM limit and T is the limit of t, and explicitly recommends the displayed
finite estimate ([author's full paper](https://maths-people.anu.edu.au/~brent/pd/rpb034.pdf)).
It follows the elliptic identities in Section 4. The
[author also credits Salamin's independent discovery](https://maths-people.anu.edu.au/~brent/pub/pub034.html).
The paper's precision-based stopping and variable working precision differ from
this library's fixed-count double model. This is an AGM π algorithm, not
Gauss–Legendre quadrature for numerical integration.

> Rerun the original six-step example without a fabricated error floor

The original program subtracts a double literal for π. That compares two rounded
floating numbers; it is not an exact real-number error. The native helper keeps
that subtraction and separately compares with std::numbers::pi_v<long double>.
The [C++ mathematical constants specification](https://eel.is/c++draft/numbers)
uses the nearest representable value of π in the selected type. Long double may
add precision on this platform, but that reference is rounded too and may have
no extra bits on other platforms. These differences are diagnostics, not certified
error bounds. The plot includes only available positive differences; it never
replaces zero with a made-up floor.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/legendre_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gauss_legendre_pi.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto t=dm::algorithms::gauss_legendre_pi_trace(6);
 std::vector<double>x0,x1,y0,y1;
 std::print("| stage | actual estimate | error to double reference | error to wider reference | offset in double-reference ULPs |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(const auto&e:t.steps){const auto a=dm::algorithms::pi_reference_accuracy(e.estimate);std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.6e} | {:.6e} | {:.1f} |\n",e.iteration,e.estimate,a.rounded_absolute_error,a.wider_absolute_error,a.rounded_ulp_offset);if(a.has_rounded_log_error){x0.push_back(e.iteration);y0.push_back(a.rounded_log10_error);}if(a.has_wider_log_error){x1.push_back(e.iteration);y1.push_back(double(a.wider_log10_error));}}
 const auto accuracy=dm::algorithms::pi_reference_accuracy(t.run.value);
 std::print("\nDouble significand bits {}; wider significand bits {}; wider reference adds bits {}.\n",std::numeric_limits<double>::digits,std::numeric_limits<long double>::digits,accuracy.wider_has_more_bits);
 std::print("First unchanged estimate stage {}; first unchanged (a,b,t) stage {}; final estimate {:.17g}.\n",t.run.first_unchanged_estimate,t.run.first_unchanged_mean_state,t.run.value);
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.lines(x0,y0,"Double reference",{37,99,235},2).points(x0,y0,"",{37,99,235},4)
  .lines(x1,y1,"Wider reference",{220,38,38},2).points(x1,y1,"",{220,38,38},4).legend({"Double reference","Wider reference"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
  .title("Actual reference differences fall, then reach a rounding plateau").xlab("Executed stage").ylab("log10 absolute reference difference; no clipped floor").y_limits(-18,0).size(1100,540).save_svg("build/legendre_original.svg");
 if(t.run.first_unchanged_estimate<0||!std::isfinite(t.run.value))throw std::runtime_error("reference plateau");
}
